// ============================================================
// 10. WRITTEN ANSWERS
// ============================================================

// 1. Explain every denied operation and identify the missing
//    privilege/action.
// The operations returned different results because we assigned
// different permissions to each role when creating the users.
// For example, our custom frontdesk role had only find, update,
// and insert permissions on the users and appointments collections.
// Therefore, the receptionist could search for, update, and insert
// documents, but could not delete documents or create indexes.
// The clinicApp user also demonstrated how permissions change when
// roles are revoked and granted. After removing readWrite, the user
// could no longer insert documents. After granting read, the user
// could read data but still could not insert documents.

// 2. Explain any differences between predictions and actual
//    results.
// The differences between our predictions and actual results
// occurred when authorization was disabled in the `mongod_lab.conf`
//  configuration file.
// After setting `authorization: enabled`, MongoDB started checking
// each user's assigned roles and permissions before allowing database operations.
// When authorization was disabled, MongoDB did not enforce role-based
// access restrictions. As a result, users could perform database operations
// even if their assigned roles did not grant them the required permissions.
// After enabling authorization, the actual results matched our permission
// matrix predictions.

// 3. How can access to patients' allergies be restricted
//    for frontdesk users?
// We can restrict access to patients' allergies by storing sensitive information
//  in a separate collection and assigning permissions to different users. For
// example, frontdesk users can have access to general patient information, while
// access to the collection containing allergies is granted only to authorized users.
// Another option is to create a view that excludes sensitive fields.

// 4. Explain the difference between a MongoDB database role
//    and the "role" field in a doctor document.
//They are different concepts. A MongoDB database role is a set of permissions that
// can be assigned to one or more database users. Users assigned the same role
// receive the permissions defined by that role.
// In contrast, the `role` field in a doctor document is just a regular data
//  field used to describe the doctor's position or function in the system.
// It does not grant database permissions.
// ============================================================
